N2  - View Video Presentation: https://doi.org/10.2514/6.2023-1881.vidIn late 2026, sci_Zone in partnership with John Hopkins University is planning to deploy in Low Earth Orbit the EarthScan-TSS satellite to test on orbit servicing and space sustainability for the DoD, NASA and a range of commercial applications. The focus of the mission is to flight qualify a range of sensors and supporting software, Artificial Intelligence, and Autonomy tools ? QuickSAT/VMS and SHERLOCK MD ? that supports vehicle servicing and fault management, extravehicular assessment, local and global situational awareness and secondary vehicle characterization. The architecture will track data in an on board data lake using a hybrid Trustless Ledger System. For ease of payload repurposing EarthScan-TSS will utilize the flight qualified QuickSAT/Xen Space Hypervisor, a bare metal hypervisor that provides for secure isolation and protection of virtual payloads installed on on-orbit vehicles. QuickSAT/Xen also allows for payload repurposing isolated from the rest of the flight management system. The first phase of the mission is for two years in LEO at an altitude of 600 km to exercise a range of mission goals including a broad range of servicing and autonomy functions. The second phase of the mission will be to deploy a 24U cubesat from the main satellite on a 2 km tether to test a range of secondary vehicle monitoring functions over the controlled deployment and tether operations phases. We will also conduct a de-orbit maneuver using sci_Zone?s proprietary tether design and tether deployer system. For this presentation the mission design will be presented along with the baseline satellite design including both the primary and secondary elements plus the tether system. We will also cover the architecture of the QuickSAT/VMS system, SHERLOCK MD, and QuickSAT/Xen.
